{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"and why is HaShem bringing us to this land to fall by the sword — our wives and our little ones\"</b> — they do not mention the evil report about the land, saying further \"and the land consumes its inhabitants,\" because the people would conceal this matter from Moses. For the spies did not say this when they gave their report to him and to all the congregation, because Moses and Aaron would testify against them that they had spoken falsely. And the spies themselves concealed this from Moses, knowing that he was acquainted with the affairs of the land from Egypt and from Midian, its neighbors, as I have explained (Numbers 14:2). Therefore they would utter this evil report to the people in their tents, secretly. Now Moses said in Deuteronomy: \"And they brought us back word and said, 'Good is the land that HaShem our Elohim gives us,' but you would not go up and you rebelled against the mouth of HaShem\" (Deuteronomy 1:25–26). The reason is that they did say to him and to Aaron and to all the congregation that the land was good and also flows with milk and honey, but the people rebelled against the mouth of HaShem when they refused to go up because of their saying that the people are fierce, and it is in HaShem's power to help or to thwart, as he explains there: \"HaShem your Elohim who goes before you — He will fight for you\" (Deuteronomy 1:30), for since the only obstacle was the might of the people, they should have trusted in the Name of HaShem, for the battle is HaShem's. And there is no need to say that Moses said this only concerning Joshua and Caleb, for why would they listen to the two and not believe the ten? Moreover, there it is written: \"And I took from you twelve men\" (Deuteronomy 1:23), and concerning all of them he said: \"And they brought us back word\" (ibid. 25)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'and it is in HaShem's power to help or to thwart,' the Hebrew reads literally 'and there is in HaShem to help and to cause to fail,' an idiom rendered here as the capacity or power to do either."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:04.433262277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}